<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ruvuma River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rovuma River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ruvuma, called the Rovuma in Portuguese, is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, about 800 km long, that rises in southern Tanzania and flows east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. For most of its course it forms the border between Tanzania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, agreed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Portugal in 1886. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> explored its lower course in 1861–1862.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ruvuma-river.jpg" alt="Ruvuma River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Livingstone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Near_the_Cataracts_of_the_Rovuma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Ruvuma, formerly also called the Rovuma, lies in the African Great Lakes region. Its drainage basin covers about 155,000 square kilometres. The lower river is formed at 11° 25′ S, 38° 31′ E by the junction of two branches of nearly equal importance: the Lujenda, which comes from the south-west, and a western branch that still bears the name Ruvuma.</p><p>The Ruvuma's source lies on an undulating plateau about 1,000 m high immediately east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Nyasa</a>. The head-stream flows first due west before turning south and east. In its eastward course the river runs near the base of the escarpment of an arid sandstone plateau to the north. The streams from that direction have cut deep channels in the plateau edge and almost all have short courses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On its southern bank the Ruvuma receives the Lujenda, the Msinje River and the Luchulingo River. The Lujenda rises in the small Lake Chiuta, whose swamps are separated from Lake Chilwa only by a narrow wooded ridge. Its stream passes through a swampy valley into the narrow Lake Amaramba, from which the Lujenda issues about 73 m wide.</p><p>Lower down, the Lujenda varies greatly in breadth and holds long wooded islands that rise above the flood level and are often inhabited. In many places it can be forded in the dry season, and at its mouth it is about 1.6 km wide. The lower Ruvuma is often 0.8 km wide but generally shallow, and runs through a swampy valley between plateau escarpments. The mouth is near 10° 28′ S, 40° 30′ E.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>For the greater part of its course the river forms the border between Tanzania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. A bridge across it, called Unity Bridge, was proposed as early as 1975 and begun, but abandoned for lack of funds. After a formal agreement in 2002 a new bridge of 600 metres was opened on 25 May 2010 at Negomano, at a cost of US$28 million.</p><p>Presidents Machel and Nyerere chose the site because Frelimo forces first entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> there on 25 September 1964, in the Mozambican War of Independence. German forces had also crossed the river at Negomano on 25 November 1917. A smaller bridge, Unity Two, was completed in 2007 on the upper river near Matchedge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niassa-province">Niassa Province</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ruvuma River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…negotiated by Alfred Milner and Pierre Orts, Britain ceded the north-western districts of Ruanda and Urundi to Belgium; on 12 July the Commission on Mandates gave the Kionga Triangle south of the <b>Rovuma River</b> to Portugal, reasoning that Germany had virtually forced Portugal to cede it in 1894.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…the headquarters of Portuguese power in East Africa, its capture by Omani forces in 1698 after a long siege ended Portuguese rule north of the <b>Ruvuma</b>, and the town later became the base of the Mazrui governors and, under British rule, the starting point of the Uganda Railway.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Lacking the money to occupy northern Mozambique, Portugal granted a charter in 1891 to the Companhia do Niassa, covering the land between the <b>Rovuma</b> and Lúrio rivers from the coast to Lake Malawi.</blockquote><a href="/state/niassa-company">Niassa Company</a></li></ul></section>
